> ld(x: f32 in 1..2, k: u32 in 0..20) = x * f32(1 << k)
> find ld 1
find ld: rewrites by identities the model prices lower (cost 6 as written, under gt1030)
  cost 2   asfloat(asint(x) + (i32(k) << 23))       x*f32(1 << k) as an add of k to the exponent field of x (derived)
      holds where x in [1, 2] (normal, never zero), k in [0, 20], and 2 * 2^20 < 2^128
      x = m * 2^e with 1 <= |m| < 2 and e >= -126: its bits hold e + 127 in the exponent field (bits 23..30)
      x * 2^k = m * 2^(e + k) has the same mantissa and sign, and e + k <= 127: it is an f32, so the multiply does not round, and its exponent field is e + 127 + k
      adding k << 23 to the bits adds k to that field; the field stays in [1, 254], so nothing carries into the sign
  cost 4   x*asfloat(k + 127 << 23)                 f32(1 << k) as its bits, the exponent field k + 127 (derived)
      holds where k in [0, 20]: 1 << k is 2^k, not wrapped
      2^k is a power of two below 2^128: an f32, so the conversion is exact
      its bits are sign 0, exponent field k + 127 (in [127, 147], a normal number) and mantissa 0: (k + 127) << 23
  cost 5   x*(asfloat(1 << k | 0x4b000000) - 8388608) the conversion f32(1 << k) by the bits of 2^23 (derived)
      holds where 1 << k in [1, 1048576], inside [0, 2^23)
      0 <= (1 << k) < 2^23: (1 << k) | 0x4B000000 is the bit pattern with sign 0, exponent field 150 and mantissa (1 << k), the f32 2^23 + (1 << k) exactly (one unit in the last place there is 1)
      2^23 + (1 << k) - 2^23 = (1 << k) is an integer below 2^24, an f32: the subtraction does not round
      f32(1 << k) is exact below 2^24: both are (1 << k)
